diff --git a/services/console/docs/API.md b/services/console/docs/API.md
index 20bbb3e3..fd51c0b0 100644
--- a/services/console/docs/API.md
+++ b/services/console/docs/API.md
@@ -1577,6 +1577,14 @@ When the sandbox principal is a Slack channel, the endpoints manage that channel
 
 The admin API also exposes `GET /api/v1/scheduled_tasks/:id` for scheduled workflows to read the task's current ID, enabled state, and delivery channel. It requires an active admin API key, returns `404` for deleted tasks, and sets `Cache-Control: no-store`.
 
+Trusted Slack ingress may call `POST /api/v1/scheduled_tasks/:id/cancel` with
+`data: { team_id, user_id, channel_id }` and the same admin API authentication.
+Only the active task author's resolved Slack identity can cancel, from the task's
+delivery channel or their DM for a user-addressed delivery. Success returns only
+`data: { id, enabled: false }`, including repeated cancellation. It stops future
+runs without deleting the task or interrupting an already dispatched execution.
+Wrong owner, workspace or destination returns `403`; missing tasks return `404`.
+
 Responses include the task's optional cron expression, fixed timezone, human-readable schedule, enabled state, next run time, and latest run metadata. The `centaur-console` CLI exposes the same operations through `tasks`, `task`, `create-task`, `update-task`, `delete-task`, and `run-task`. Omit `--cron` when creating a manual-only task, or pass `update-task --cron ""` to remove an existing recurring schedule.
 
